In C++, try_lock() and owns_lock() are commonly used with std::unique_lock to perform non-blocking mutex acquisition and
to check whether the lock was successfully acquired.

1. try_lock()

try_lock() attempts to acquire the mutex without blocking.

Returns true if the mutex was successfully locked.
Returns false if another thread already owns the mutex.
Unlike lock(), it does not wait for the mutex to become available.
Example
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx;

int main() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx, std::defer_lock);

    if (lock.try_lock()) {
        std::cout << "Lock acquired\n";
        // Critical section
    } else {
        std::cout << "Failed to acquire lock\n";
    }

    return 0;
}

What happens?
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx, std::defer_lock);

Creates a unique_lock.
Does not lock the mutex yet because of std::defer_lock.
lock.try_lock();

Attempts to lock the mutex immediately.
Returns true or false.
2. owns_lock()

owns_lock() checks whether the unique_lock currently owns the mutex.

Returns true if lock acquisition succeeded.
Returns false otherwise.
Example
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx;

int main() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx, std::try_to_lock);

    if (lock.owns_lock()) {
        std::cout << "I own the mutex\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "Mutex is busy\n";
    }

    return 0;
}

Real-world Example (Two Threads)
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <chrono>

std::mutex mtx;

void worker1()
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lg(mtx);

    std::cout << "Thread1 acquired lock\n";

    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(5));
}

void worker2()
{
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));

    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> ul(mtx, std::defer_lock);

    if (ul.try_lock()) {
        std::cout << "Thread2 got lock\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "Thread2 failed to get lock\n";
    }

    std::cout << "owns_lock = "
              << std::boolalpha
              << ul.owns_lock()
              << std::endl;
}

int main()
{
    std::thread t1(worker1);
    std::thread t2(worker2);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
}

Output
Thread1 acquired lock
Thread2 failed to get lock
owns_lock = false

Why?

Timeline:

T1 ---- lock mutex ---------- sleep(5s) -------- unlock

T2       try_lock()
         |
         +--> mutex busy
               return false


Since Thread1 already owns the mutex, try_lock() in Thread2 fails immediately and owns_lock() returns false.

Common AAOS / Android Pattern

When you don't want a Binder thread or UI thread to block:

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mMutex, std::try_to_lock);

if (!lock.owns_lock()) {
    ALOGW("Resource busy");
    return;
}

// Safe access to shared data
processData();


This pattern is frequently used for:

Vehicle HAL callbacks
Binder services
UI event handling
Logging/statistics paths

where waiting on a mutex could hurt responsiveness.

Difference between lock(), try_lock(), and owns_lock()
Function	          Blocks?	             Return Value	                  Purpose      
lock()	               Yes	                  void	                           Wait until mutex is acquired
try_lock()	            No	                  bool	                           Attempt lock once
owns_lock()	            No	                  bool	                           Check whether unique_lock 
                                                                               currently owns mutex
Typical Flow
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx, std::defer_lock);

if (lock.try_lock()) {
    // acquired
}

if (lock.owns_lock()) {
    // safe to use shared resource
}


Think of it as:

try_lock()  -> Attempt to get the key
owns_lock() -> Check whether you actually got the key
